EJECT(1)	    UNIX Programmer's Manual		 EJECT(1)



NAME
     eject - eject media device from drive

SYNOPSIS
     eject [ -dfnq ] [ _d_e_v_i_c_e | _n_i_c_k_n_a_m_e ]

DESCRIPTION
     _e_j_e_c_t is used for those removable media devices that do
     not have a manual eject button.  The device may be
     specified by its name or by a nickname; if no device is
     specified the default device, _/_d_e_v_/_c_d_0, is used.

     Only devices that support _e_j_e_c_t under program control
     respond to this command: on FGX, the CD-ROM drives
     _/_d_e_v_/_c_d_0 to _/_d_e_v_/_c_d_3.

     It is not recommended to physically eject media from a
     device which contains mounted filesystems.  _e_j_e_c_t
     automatically searches for any mounted filesystems which
     reside on the device and attempts to unmount them prior to
     ejecting the media (see _u_m_o_u_n_t(8)).  If the unmount
     operation fails, _e_j_e_c_t prints a warning message and
     exits.

     _e_j_e_c_t can also display its default device and a list of
     nicknames.

OPTIONS
     -d	 Display the name of the default device to be ejected.

     -f	 Go on to the eject even if the device contains mounted
	 filesystems.  See BUGS.

     -n	 Display the nickname to device name translation table.

     -q	 Query to see if the media is present.

     _d_e_v_i_c_e
	 Specify which device to _e_j_e_c_t, by the name as it
	 appears in the directory _/_d_e_v.

     _n_i_c_k_n_a_m_e
	 Specify which device to _e_j_e_c_t, by its nickname as known
	 to this command: _c_d, _c_d_r_o_m, or _c_d_0 to _c_d_3.

EXAMPLES
     To change the disc in the first CD-ROM drive:

	  eject cd
	  (put the new disc in)
	  mount_isofs /dev/cd0 /mnt

     A disc changed by hand needs only an unmount first; the
     drive is asked about its new disc on the next read.

FILES
     /dev/cd0 to /dev/cd3
		    block files

EXIT STATUS
     _e_j_e_c_t returns the following exit codes:

	  0    If the operation was successful or, with the -q
	       option, if the media _i_s in the drive.

	  1    If the operation was unsuccessful or, with the -q
	       option, if the media is _n_o_t in the drive.

	  2    If invalid flags were specified, or the device
	       cannot be opened.

	  3    If an _i_o_c_t_l(2) request failed.

SEE ALSO
     mount_isofs(8), umount(8)

DIAGNOSTICS
     A short help message is printed if an unknown flag is
     specified.  A diagnostic is printed if the device name
     cannot be opened or does not support _e_j_e_c_t.

BUGS
     -f cannot eject a disc whose filesystem will not unmount:
     FGX has no raw CD-ROM device, and a block device with a
     filesystem mounted on it cannot be opened.

     There should be a way to change the default on a per-user
     basis.
